from: viz-panels planner
to: plan (atomic-step-protocol / session-coordination scope)
session: shared-git-index-incident

## Incident, for the record — a real gap in session-coordination conventions

2026-08-16: a viz-panels planner session (`git add planning/benchmark-runs-inventory.md && git
commit ...`) produced commit `f9e1dba6` on `plans`, which unintentionally swept in 4 unrelated
`scripts/*` files (`scripts/lib/single-instance-guard.sh` new + 3 edits) that a different,
concurrent session (a coder working `single-instance-guard`) had staged moments earlier with its
own `git add`.

**Root cause, independently confirmed by both sessions:** the `plans` worktree is shared by
multiple concurrent sessions, and they all share **one git index** (staging area). Any `git add`
leaves files staged in that shared index; whichever session commits *next* — from either side —
sweeps up everything currently staged, not just its own files. Neither session violated an
existing rule: `CONVENTIONS.md` gives explicit single-writer/ownership protocols for CURRENT.md,
handoffs, and status files, but never named the git index itself as shared state needing the same
care. The full incident account, from the side that found it via a failed commit, is in
`session/handoffs/plan__shared-git-index-commit-collision.md` (still open, not yet folded into any
convention doc).

## Resolution — Dean's ruling, verbatim intent

**Accept the git-index collision risk; document the mitigation; do not rewrite history to fix
`f9e1dba6`.** Dean's own framing: "the commits are more for persistence than scoping, but they are
heavily used by each session to scope their work, so good solution. If collisions happen despite
proposed precautions, we accept it and we know how to recover." In other words: commits in `plans`
are not strict provenance records — they're working checkpoints sessions use to orient themselves —
so an occasional misattributed commit is a tolerable, recoverable cost, not something worth a
history rewrite (which itself risks disrupting another session's in-flight work on a shared branch).

**The mitigation to adopt going forward** (found and tested by the coder who hit this):

```
git commit -s -m "..." -- <path> [<path> ...]
```

Pathspec-only commits — never a bare `git add` followed by `git commit`, never `git commit -a`,
never `git restore --staged` someone else's staged file to "clean up." A pathspec commit only
touches the exact paths named and leaves nothing behind in the shared index for another session's
commit to sweep up. **Caveat, found by testing:** a pathspec commit can't reach a brand-new
(untracked) file — git errors "did not match any file(s) known to git" — so new files still need
`git add` first; the safe pattern there is `git add <path> && git commit -m ... -- <path>` as one
shell invocation, minimizing the window the new file sits staged.

## Why this lands with your scope

This is a session-coordination/process convention, structurally the same class of thing the
atomic-step-protocol design already owns (checkpoint capture, handoff mechanics, multi-session
coordination in `plans`). Flagging for your awareness and, if you judge it warranted, folding into
`CONVENTIONS.md` § Key Working Rules or wherever else session-coordination rules already live — not
mine to decide where it belongs, just surfacing it so it isn't only captured in two handoff files
that will eventually get cleaned up.

## Not part of this handoff

- `f9e1dba6` itself is not being touched — per Dean's ruling above.
- The other coder's own two additional findings in the same handoff sweep (Tier-1 capture marker
  poisoning, `sync-session` capture never appending) are a **separate, unrelated** issue — real,
  live defects in checkpoint capture, not the git-index incident. Not restated here; see
  `session/handoffs/plan__tier1-capture-marker-poisoning.md` directly if this scope doesn't already
